                                <span class="text-faint group-hover:text-fg transition-colors">↗</span>
                            </span>
                            <span class="text-sm text-muted">{l.text}</span>
                        </a>
                    ))}
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================== ECS AND PARALLELISM ============================== -->
    <section id="ecs" class="border-y border-line bg-surface scroll-mt-16">
        <div class="wrap py-20 md:py-28">
            <p class="eyebrow mb-4">Data</p>
            <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight leading-[1.15]">Data-oriented ECS and parallelism</h2>
            <p class="mt-8 max-w-2xl body text-[17px]">
                Synapse Engine runs on its own sparse-set based Entity-Component-System. Entities are plain IDs, components
                are plain data, and every component type lives in its own tightly packed pool.
            </p>

            <div class="mt-12 rounded-lg border border-line bg-bg p-6 md:p-10">
                <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
                    <SparseSet />
                </div>
            </div>

            <dl class="mt-12 grid gap-x-8 gap-y-8 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
                {ecsProps.map((p) => (
                    <div class="border-t border-line pt-4">
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-fg mb-1">{p.title}</dt>
                        <dd class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">{p.text}</dd>
                    </div>
                ))}
            </dl>

            <!-- Segments -->
            <div class="mt-24">
                <h3 class="h-sub mb-4">Static, dynamic and stream</h3>
                <p class="body max-w-2xl mb-10">
                    A plain sparse set, like the one in EnTT, was not enough for a GPU-driven pipeline. Walking millions of
                    components costs time even when nothing changed. So every pool is split into three segments.
                </p>
                <div class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-bg p-6 md:p-8">
                    <Segments />
                </div>
                <div class="mt-6 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-3">
                    {ecsSegments.map((s) => (
                        <div class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-bg p-6">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
                                <span class:list={['h-3 w-3 rounded-sm border', `swatch-${s.key}`]}></span>
                                <h4 class="font-semibold">{s.name}</h4>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">{s.text}</p>
                        </div>
                    ))}
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- EnTT -->
            <div class="mt-24 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-12 items-start">
                <div class="lg:col-span-5">
                    <h3 class="h-sub mb-4">Compared with EnTT</h3>
                    <p class="body">
                        Adding components is faster in EnTT. Removing them and the full engine update are faster in Synapse,
                        because unchanged data is skipped.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="lg:col-span-7 min-w-0">
                    <div class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-bg min-w-0">
                        <div class="px-6 pt-5 pb-1 text-sm font-semibold">1 million entities</div>
                        <div class="px-6 pb-2 overflow-x-auto">
                            <table class="data-table">
                                <thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th class="num">Synapse</th><th class="num">EnTT</th></tr></thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    {ecsBench.map((r, i) => (
                                        <tr class:list={[r.op === 'Full engine update' && 'row-strong', i === ecsBench.length - 1 && 'last-row']}>
                                            <td>{r.op}</td>
                                            <td class="num">{ms(r.s, 2)}</td>
                                            <td class="num">{ms(r.e, 2)}</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    ))}
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Go deeper -->
            <div class="mt-20">
                <h3 class="h-sub mb-6">Want to go deeper?</h3>
                <div class="grid gap-px bg-line border border-line rounded-lg overflow-hidden md:grid-cols-2">
                    {ecsLinks.map((l) => (
                        <a href={l.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="group bg-bg p-6 hover:bg-surface transition-colors flex flex-col">
                            <span class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-faint mb-4">{l.kind}</span>
                            <span class="text-fg font-medium mb-1 flex items-center justify-between gap-3">
                                {l.title}
                                <span class="text-faint group-hover:text-fg transition-colors">↗</span>
                            </span>
                            <span class="text-sm text-muted">{l.text}</span>
                        </a>
                    ))}
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================== ARCHITECTURE AND CLEAN CODE ============================== -->
    <section id="architecture" class="scroll-mt-16">
        <div class="wrap py-20 md:py-28">
            <p class="eyebrow mb-4">Architecture</p>
            <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight leading-[1.15]">Readable first, and still fast</h2>

            <div class="mt-10 grid gap-x-12 gap-y-5 md:grid-cols-2 body text-[17px]">
                <p>
                    The goal was a codebase people can actually read. SOLID principles, clean code and clear layers, without
                    giving up performance.
                </p>
                <p>
                    For research this matters a lot. If you can't find an algorithm in the code, you can't change it or
                    measure it either.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Model pipeline -->
            <div class="mt-20">
                <h3 class="h-sub mb-4">Example: the model pipeline</h3>
                <p class="body max-w-2xl mb-10">
                    A model goes through clear stages, each with its own representation. Every step is a small class behind an
                    interface, so a new format or processing step never touches the rest.
                </p>
                <ol class="grid gap-x-6 gap-y-8 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
                    {modelStages.map((s, i) => (
                        <li class="border-t border-fg pt-5">
                            <span class="font-mono text-xs text-faint">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
                            <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold mt-2 mb-2">{s.name}</h4>
                            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">{s.text}</p>
                        </li>
                    ))}
                </ol>
                <Figure class="mt-12" src={`${IMG}/Architecture_ModelPipeline.png`} alt="Class diagram of the layered model pipeline">
                    The full model pipeline. Sources, processors, converters and uploaders, each behind its own interface.
                </Figure>
            </div>

            <!-- Go deeper -->
            <div class="mt-20">
                <h3 class="h-sub mb-6">Want to go deeper?</h3>
                <div class="grid gap-px bg-line border border-line rounded-lg overflow-hidden md:grid-cols-2">
                    {archLinks.map((l) => (
                        <a href={l.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="group bg-bg p-6 hover:bg-surface transition-colors flex flex-col">
                            <span class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-faint mb-4">{l.kind}</span>
                            <span class="text-fg font-medium mb-1 flex items-center justify-between gap-3">
                                {l.title}
                                <span class="text-faint group-hover:text-fg transition-colors">↗</span>
                            </span>
                            <span class="text-sm text-muted">{l.text}</span>
                        </a>
                    ))}
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================== MVI AND EDITOR ============================== -->
    <section id="editor" class="border-y border-line bg-surface scroll-mt-16">
        <div class="wrap py-20 md:py-28">
            <p class="eyebrow mb-4">Editor</p>
            <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight leading-[1.15]">MVI architecture and the editor</h2>

            <div class="mt-10 grid gap-x-12 gap-y-5 md:grid-cols-2 body text-[17px]">
                <p>
                    The editor follows Model-View-Intent. The UI never changes engine state directly. When you press a button or
                    edit a value, the view sends an intent, and the ViewModel in EditorCore decides what happens.
                </p>
                <p>
                    This keeps the editor logic independent from the UI framework. Today the views are written in ImGui. Moving to
                    another framework would mean rewriting the views only, while all the editor logic stays the same.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="mt-14 rounded-lg border border-line bg-bg p-6 md:p-8">
                <div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-[1fr_auto_1fr_auto_1fr] items-stretch">
                    {editorLayers.map((l, i) => (
                        <>
                            <div class="rounded-md border border-line bg-surface p-5">
                                <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-faint mb-2">{l.role}</p>
                                <p class="font-semibold mb-1">{l.name}</p>
                                <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">{l.text}</p>
                            </div>
                            {i < editorLayers.length - 1 && (
                                <div class="flex md:flex-col items-center justify-center gap-1 font-mono text-xs text-faint py-1">
                                    <span>{i === 0 ? 'intent →' : 'calls →'}</span>
                                    <span>{i === 0 ? '← state' : '← sync'}</span>
                                </div>
                            )}
                        </>
                    ))}
                </div>
            </div>
            <Figure class="mt-6" src={`${IMG}/Architecture_MVI.png`} alt="MVI layered architecture of the editor">
                The detailed MVI structure of the editor.
            </Figure>

            <!-- Editors -->
            <div class="mt-24">
                <h3 class="h-sub mb-4">Workspaces</h3>
                <p class="body max-w-2xl mb-10">
                    Every asset type has its own workspace, so scenes, models, materials, textures, animations and sounds can be
                    handled inside the engine without external tools.
                </p>
                <Figure src={`${IMG}/Editor_Scene.png`} alt="Scene workspace of the Synapse Engine editor">
                    <b>Scene.</b> Entities and components, renderer settings, profiling and the asset browser.
                </Figure>
                <div class="mt-10 grid gap-x-6 gap-y-10 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-5">
                    {editors.map((e) => (
                        <div>
                            <Figure src={`${IMG}/${e.img}`} crop alt={`${e.name} workspace`} />
                            <h4 class="mt-4 font-semibold mb-1">{e.name}</h4>
                            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">{e.text}</p>
                        </div>
                    ))}
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Go deeper -->
            <div class="mt-20">
                <h3 class="h-sub mb-6">Want to go deeper?</h3>
                <div class="grid gap-px bg-line border border-line rounded-lg overflow-hidden md:grid-cols-2">
                    {editorLinks.map((l) => (
                        <a href={l.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="group bg-bg p-6 hover:bg-surface transition-colors flex flex-col">
                            <span class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-faint mb-4">{l.kind}</span>
                            <span class="text-fg font-medium mb-1 flex items-center justify-between gap-3">
                                {l.title}
                                <span class="text-faint group-hover:text-fg transition-colors">↗</span>
                            </span>
                            <span class="text-sm text-muted">{l.text}</span>
                        </a>
                    ))}
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================== MODERN HARDWARE ONLY ============================== -->
    <section id="requirements" class="scroll-mt-16">
        <div class="wrap py-20 md:py-28">
            <p class="eyebrow mb-4">Requirements</p>
            <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight leading-[1.15]">Built for modern hardware only</h2>

            <div class="mt-10 grid gap-x-12 gap-y-5 md:grid-cols-2 body text-[17px]">
                <p>
                    Synapse Engine is made for research. New rendering ideas need the newest graphics API and the newest GPU
                    architectures, so the engine is built around them from the start.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Supporting old hardware would close the door on exactly the features that GPU-driven techniques rely on.
                    So the engine will never support older GPUs. Only modern architectures.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="mt-12 rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface">
                {requirements.map((g, gi) => (
                    <div class:list={[gi > 0 && 'border-t border-line']}>
                        <div class="px-6 pt-6 pb-2 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-faint">{g.group}</div>
                        <dl class="px-6 pb-3">
                            {g.rows.map(([k, v], i) => (
                                <div class:list={['grid gap-1 sm:grid-cols-[11rem_1fr] sm:gap-6 py-3 text-sm', i > 0 && 'border-t border-line']}>
                                    <dt class="text-fg font-medium">{k}</dt>
                                    <dd class="text-muted">{v}</dd>
                                </div>
                            ))}
                        </dl>
                    </div>
                ))}
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================== PUBLICATIONS AND TALKS ============================== -->
    <section id="publications" class="border-y border-line bg-surface scroll-mt-16">
        <div class="wrap py-20 md:py-28">
            <p class="eyebrow mb-4">Publications</p>
            <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight leading-[1.15]">Publications and talks</h2>
            <p class="mt-8 max-w-2xl body text-[17px]">
                Some of these are in Hungarian, my native language. Feel free to run them through a translator, and look at the
                figures too. They tell a lot on their own.
            </p>

            <ul class="mt-12 rounded-lg border border-line bg-bg overflow-hidden">
                {talks.map((t, i) => {
                    const inner = (
                        <>
                            <span class="font-mono text-xs text-faint sm:w-20 shrink-0 pt-0.5">{t.date}</span>
                            <span class="flex-1 min-w-0">
                                <span class="block text-fg text-[15px] leading-snug">{t.title}</span>
                                <span class="block text-sm text-faint mt-1">{t.kind} · {t.venue}</span>
                            </span>
                            <span class="flex items-center gap-3 shrink-0">
                                <span class:list={['font-mono text-[11px] rounded border px-1.5 py-0.5', t.lang === 'EN' ? 'border-fg/40 text-fg' : 'border-line text-muted']}>{t.lang}</span>
                                {t.href
                                    ? <span class="sm:w-20 text-right text-faint group-hover:text-fg transition-colors">↗</span>
                                    : <span class="sm:w-20 text-right font-mono text-[11px] text-faint">coming</span>}
                            </span>
                        </>
                    );
                    return (
                        <li class:list={[i > 0 && 'border-t border-line']}>
                            {t.href
                                ? <a href={t.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="group flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-start gap-2 sm:gap-6 px-6 py-5 hover:bg-surface transition-colors">{inner}</a>
                                : <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-start gap-2 sm:gap-6 px-6 py-5">{inner}</div>}
                        </li>
                    );
                })}
            </ul>
            <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-faint">The theses will be linked here once they are finished.</p>

            <!-- Go deeper -->
            <div class="mt-20">
                <h3 class="h-sub mb-6">Want to go deeper?</h3>
                <a href="/devblog/01-the-synapse-journey" class="group block rounded-lg border border-line bg-bg p-6 hover:bg-surface transition-colors md:max-w-[50%]">
                    <span class="block font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-faint mb-4">Devblog</span>
                    <span class="text-fg font-medium mb-1 flex items-center justify-between gap-3">
                        Dev diary
                        <span class="text-faint group-hover:text-fg transition-colors">→</span>
                    </span>
                    <span class="block text-sm text-muted">
                        The messages between me and my supervisor. Progress updates, ideas and a lot of thinking about algorithms.
                    </span>
                </a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================== AI USE ============================== -->
    <section id="ai" class="scroll-mt-16">
        <div class="wrap py-20 md:py-28">
            <p class="eyebrow mb-4">Transparency</p>
            <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight leading-[1.15]">How I use AI</h2>

            <p class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-2xl md:text-[1.75rem] leading-snug font-medium tracking-tight text-fg">
                Not a single line of code or idea went into this engine that I have not reviewed, that I don't understand,
                or that I couldn't explain.
            </p>

            <div class="mt-12 grid gap-x-12 gap-y-5 md:grid-cols-2 body text-[17px]">
                <div class="space-y-5">
                    <p>
                        I used AI while building Synapse Engine, mostly Google Gemini Pro through the student plan. I use it for
                        almost everything around the thinking. Brainstorming ideas, finding papers and techniques, seeing how other
                        engines solve a problem, planning the architecture and helping to write parts of the code.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        The mental model of the whole engine and every decision in it live in my head. That is the only reason I
                        can keep moving forward. Every part is connected to every other part, and no AI keeps track of that on its own.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="space-y-5">
                    <p>
                        I have been building engines for three to four years. My BSc thesis, Dendrite Engine, was a fairly large
                        engine written before AI tools existed, and I spent countless hours learning computer graphics the hard way.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        I think that is exactly why AI works for me. I know what to ask, I can read the answers critically, and
                        after a while I can feel which algorithm will work and which won't. AI gives me more information and more
                        ideas. The understanding and the decisions are still mine.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================== AUTHOR ============================== -->
    <section class="wrap">
        <div class="border-t border-line pt-10 flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-center gap-6 justify-between">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-5">
                <img src="/Images/Profile.png" alt="Tamás Péter" class="h-14 w-14 rounded-full object-cover border border-line" />
                <div>
                    <p class="font-semibold">Tamás Péter</p>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted">Computer Engineering MSc, Visual Informatics, BME. Supervisor: András Máté Fridvalszky.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <a href="/about" class="btn-secondary">About me</a>
        </div>
    </section>

</Layout>

